
<section xml:id="homework-1">
    <subsection xml:id="affine-span">
      <title>Affine Span</title>

      <p>The <term>affine span</term> <m>\text{aff}(S)</m> of a set of vectors <m>S</m>
       is the set of all finite linear combinations of vectors in <m>S</m> such that the
        coefficients sum to <m>1</m>. Such a linear combination is called 
        an <term>affine</term> linear combination. For example, 
        <m>2(1,0) + 3(0,1) + -4(1,1) = (2,3) -(4,4) = (-2,-1)</m> is an affine combination 
        of <m>(1,0), (0,1)</m>, and <m>(1,1)</m>.</p>

      <exercise xml:id="exercise-affine-span-two-points">
        <statement>
          <p>Let <m>(1,0), (0,1) \in \mathbb{R}^{2}</m>. Draw and describe <m>\text{aff}((1,0),(0,1))</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p/>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="exercise-affine-span-zero-in-s">
        <statement>
          <p>Let <m>S \subseteq V</m> for a vector space <m>V</m>, and suppose that <m>0 \in S</m>. Show that <m>\text{aff}(S) = \text{span}(S)</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p/>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <p>An <term>affine subspace</term> is a subset of a vector space <m>V</m> such that any affine linear combination of finitely many elements in <m>S</m> is still in <m>S</m>.</p>

      <exercise xml:id="exercise-affine-span-subspace">
        <statement>
          <p>Show that for any subset <m>S \subseteq V</m>, <m>\text{aff}(S)</m> is an affine linear subspace.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p/>
        </solution>
      </exercise>


    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="affine-independence">
      <title>Affine Independence</title>

      <p>A finite set of vectors is <term>affinely independent</term> if there is no nontrivial linear combination of them that sums to <m>0</m> such that the coefficients sum to <m>0</m>. For example <m>(0,2), (2,0), (1,1)</m> are not affinely independent, because</p>
      <md>1(0,2) + 1(2,0) -2(1,1) = (0,0),</md>
      <p>and <m>1 + 1 -2 = 0</m>.</p>

      <exercise xml:id="exercise-zero-affinely-independent">
        <statement>
          <p>Show that <m>\{\mathbf{0}\}</m> is affinely independent.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p/>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="exercise-independent-proper-subset">
        <statement>
          <p>Prove that if a set <m>S</m> of vectors is affinely independent, then <m>\text{aff}(T) \neq \text{aff}(S)</m> for any <m>T \subsetneq S</m>.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p/>
        </solution>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="exercise-proper-subset-independent">
        <statement>
          <p>Prove that if a set <m>S</m> of vectors satisfies <m>\text{aff}(T) \neq \text{aff}(S)</m> for any <m>T \subsetneq S</m>, then <m>S</m> is affinely independent.</p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p/>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>
  </section>

